{"agent":"close-brules","data":{"by":"grok-4.7","evidence":{"chunk_findings":[{"chunk":1,"detail":"`rule_inventory` accepts `agent` and then drops it: `RI.inventory(repo)` is called with only the repo. Given `rule_inventory(repo, agent=\"claude\")`, the filter never reaches the census, so the outcome is the full always-loaded inventory rather than that agent's files and byte cost. The parameter is part of the public signature and the stated per-agent inventory, so ignoring it is a wrong result, not an unused import.","digest":"b43f1b28557feb03","family":"xai","location":"ddflow/api/rulesinv.py:11","malformed":"","reviewer":"grok-4.7","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"ddflow/api/rulesinv.py:11"},{"chunk":2,"detail":"When one path is both a command-dir file and another agent’s rule read, classification and `loaded_by` depend on `HR.load()` order and contradict the “rule glob wins” comment. Instructions are emitted before that harness’s command dir, but a later harness’s `reads` does not repair a row already created as `KIND_COMMAND`: `kind` and `_load_mode` stay `command` / `on request`, so an always-loaded rule (no frontmatter, or `alwaysApply: true`) is omitted from that agent’s `always_bytes`, from `doctor_note` (it drops `KIND_COMMAND`), and from restatement notes. The opposite order hits `continue` and never records the command agent at all, so the census under-states who loads the file.","digest":"5148d85b81adf3e9","family":"xai","location":"ddflow/services/rulesinv.py:scan_rule_files","malformed":"","reviewer":"grok-4.7","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"ddflow/services/rulesinv.py:scan_rule_files"}],"chunks_total":1,"coverage":"1/1 chunk(s) reviewed","delta_findings":0,"delta_from":"d4940a6d1410f906e61afe3053b6da5eaab13785","delta_raised":[],"delta_rounds":1,"diff_chars":4313,"diff_sha":"93eece58726127ac4e7e3ba7ef353be14a37fa1b0bcf64fb03fce337bdab99e5","diff_source":"delta review of 1 commit since d4940a6d14","diff_stat":{"deletions":0,"files":0,"insertions":0,"untracke